<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kolubara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Kolubara was fought in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in November and December 1914, when the Serbian army, reorganised under Radomir Putnik and Živojin Mišić, counter-attacked an Austro-Hungarian army that had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. The Austro-Hungarians were driven out of Serbia with heavy losses, and Belgrade was retaken on 15 December.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kolubara.jpg" alt="Battle of Kolubara" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edgar Allan Poe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Kolubara.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavrilo-princip">Gavrilo Princip</a> assassinated Archduke Franz Ferdinand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> on 28 June 1914, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> issued an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-ultimatum-to-serbia">ultimatum to Serbia</a> on 23 July, designed to be unacceptable, and declared war on 28 July. Oskar Potiorek commanded the first invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in August with far fewer troops than the 308,000 intended, since much of the 2nd Army had gone to the Russian front. The Serbian army was led by Crown Prince Alexander, with Radomir Putnik as his deputy and de facto military leader.</p><p>A third invasion was authorised in early October by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> after Austro-Hungarian troops found in a bookshop a map, The New Division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, showing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> abolished. Rain and snow had swamped the valleys of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and Putnik remarked that his whole strategy was to place the Serbian national mud between the enemy's fighting line and his supplies. The invasion began on 6 November, and the outnumbered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, short of ammunition, retreated. Putnik ordered the abandonment of Valjevo and a stand on the Kolubara. Nikola Pašić telegraphed abroad for urgent help, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> supplied munitions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Austro-Hungarians reached the Kolubara on 16 November and attacked the next day. For five days both armies fought in heavy rain and snow, and many soldiers died of frostbite and hypothermia. By 19 November the Austro-Hungarians had gained a foothold at Vrače Brdo and the Serbian 1st Army was forced back.</p><p>On the night of 26 to 27 November the Austro-Hungarian 6th Army attacked along the whole front. The Serbian command abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 29 and 30 November, and the Austro-Hungarians entered on 1 December, to celebrations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Potiorek wrote that he was laying Belgrade at the emperor's feet.</p><p>But supply lines were overextended, and Potiorek paused. Živojin Mišić withdrew the Serbian 1st Army to rest, and supplies reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> by the Niš to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonika</a> railway. On 2 December Putnik ordered a counterattack along the whole front, and King Peter I took a rifle to the front. The attack caught the Austro-Hungarians holding a parade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, with their artillery well behind the line. Valjevo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzice">Užice</a> were retaken on 8 December, and the Austro-Hungarians abandoned Belgrade on 14 and 15 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>It was a decisive Serbian victory. It failed to knock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> out of the war, to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> or to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> neutral, and Austro-Hungarian historians judged it a serious loss of prestige. Potiorek and Liborius Ritter von Frank were relieved of command on 22 December. Austro-Hungarian casualties are given as about 225,000, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> lost 22,000 killed, 91,000 wounded and 19,000 missing or captured. Western correspondents reported atrocities by Austro-Hungarian troops against Serbian civilians, and William Shepard of the United Press saw at least 11 towns abandoned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kolubara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Franz Conrad von Hötzendorf's offensive planning was badly at odds with the army's real capabilities: in 1914 the invasions of Serbia were repulsed at Cer and the <b>Kolubara</b>, while in Galicia the army was routed around Lemberg (Lviv), losing much of its trained peacetime officer corps within months.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
